(a) Generally. With the exception of professional services and public-works contracts or as otherwise allowed or permitted under the Official Code of Ordinances of the City of Jesup, all purchases or acquisitions of all materials, supplies, equipment and contractual services, when the cost thereof shall exceed the amount of $20,000.00 shall be purchased or acquired under a formal, written contract with the lowest responsible and responsive bidder, after due notice inviting the submission of written bid proposals for such materials, supplies, equipment and/or contractual services; at the option of the city manager bids may also be accepted by email or may be established by the "online reverse auction" process as defined by the Georgia Municipal Association or its successor entity or similar online process. In the event that the "online reverse auction" process is used, the length of the auction and the amount of the bid increments shall be established by the city manager and shall be incorporated into the bid notice. "Public works projects" is defined as infrastructure projects, including construction, repair, remodeling, alteration, conversion, modernization, improvement, rehabilitation, replacement, or renovation, financed by the City of Jesup, for recreational, employment, and health and safety uses in the City of Jesup, Georgia.
(b) Notice inviting bids. Notice inviting bids shall also be published once in the official organ of Wayne County, Georgia, where the sheriff of said county also carries his or her advertisements, at least ten days before the last day set for the receipt of bids.
(c) Scope of notice. The newspaper notice required in subsection (b) of this section shall include a description of the materials, supplies, equipment or contractual services to be purchased, shall state where bid blanks or bid forms, if used, and the general specifications of the bid may be secured, and the time and place for opening bids.
(d) Bid deposits. When deemed necessary by the board of commissioners, bid deposits shall be prescribed in the public notices inviting bids.
(e) Bid opening procedures. Bids shall be submitted in the form and shall contain the content directed by the city, sealed to the attention of the city, and shall be identified as a "sealed bid" on the envelope. The envelope must be of sufficient weight and bulk whereby the amount of the bid may not be visibly detected outside the envelope. All bids shall be delivered to the city by certified mail, return receipt requested, or personally delivered to the city hall on the date and at the time specified by the city. All bids shall be opened with at least two members of the board of commissioners present. The city manager shall refer such bids to the appropriate department head to ensure that bid specifications have been met and to obtain a recommendation from such department head. A tabulation of all bids received shall be posted for public inspection. The city manager shall notify the board in writing of such bid openings and deliver the bids to the board along with the agenda for the regular or special meeting at which such bids may be considered. The city manager shall also make a recommendation to the board on such bids.
(f) Rejection of bids. The board of commissioners shall have the authority to reject all bids or parts of all bids. Any bid that is not in the form directed by the city or that does not contain content required by the city may be rejected, and such bidder may be considered as a non-responsive bidder and such bid as non-responsive. The board shall have the further right to waive all technicalities or requirements required under the city's bid specifications, except those provided by this article.
(g) Award of contract. Except as otherwise required or permitted under a statewide law of general jurisdiction, applicable federal law or the Code of the City of Jesup, Georgia, contracts for or purchases or acquisitions of all other materials, supplies, equipment and contractual services, with the exception of public works projects, in excess of $20,000.00 shall be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder. In determining the lowest responsible bidder, in addition to price, the board of commissioners shall consider:
(1) The ability, capacity and skill of the bidder to perform the contract;
(2) Whether the bidder can perform the contract or provide the service promptly, or within the time specified, without delay or interference;
(3) The character, integrity, reputation, judgment, experience and efficiency of the bidder;
(4) The quality of performance of previous contracts;
(5) The previous and existing compliance by the bidder with laws and ordinances relating to the contract and the bidder's prior similar work, including for other persons or entities other than the city;
(6) The sufficiency of the financial resources, credit standing, Better Business Bureau rating, Dunn and Bradstreet's rating, bonding capacity and the perceived ability of the bidder to perform the contract, to do the work or to perform the services sought by the city;
(7) The ability of the bidder to provide future maintenance for the use of the contract;
(8) The number and scope of conditions attached to the bid; and
(9) The responsiveness of the bidder.
(Ord. of 8-18-2015, § 1; Ord. of 10-4-2022(1))

